  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAnother period of extended wet weather is forecast to enter the\narea Saturday night and into Sunday morning. Ascent will mostly\nbe due to a shortwave trough and ridge breakdown in a transition\nto more quasi-zonal flow for the beginning of next week.\n\nThe first round will arrive Saturday night into Sunday with the\nbulk of the moisture axis focused over the northern forecast\narea to begin the wet period. The totals up north point to the\nHREF giving a 60% to 70% probability of exceeding 0.50\". \nHowever, the 90th percentile here shows some 1.00\" to 1.75\" 24\nhour rainfall amounts. With PWAT amounts in the 1.8 to 2.0\nrange, a 90th percentile amount is certainly more probable. The\nheaviest rainfall is expected arrive by Sunday morning in the\n12Z to 16Z timeframe. \n\nThis 24 to 36 hours period will likely consist of a few waves of\nconvection bringing heavy rainfall to the region. The other\nissue will be the MU CAPE values for Sunday tops off at around\n1000 J/Kg and this may be enough to increase the efficiency in\nthe rainfall rates. While there likely won't be enough\ninstability for severe concerns, there is quite a bit of low\nlevel shear in place. Will need to watch for an isolated spin-\nup. \n\nContinued rain chances are forecast into Monday, Tuesday, and\nWednesday, though temperatures will be cooler and rain is\nexpected to be mostly stratiform. That being said, long duration\naccumulations have the potential to be sizable, with 90th\npercentile 48hr totals of up to 2\" to 4\". While this wouldn't\npose as much a threat for flash flooding, areal flooding and\nriver flooding chances may increase should this high-end\npotential be realized. The primary way to realize high end\npotential would be to be positioned immediately north of the\nstationary boundary with overrunning and/or on the northwest \nside of a developing coastal low. Should the stationary boundary\nbe too far south or the coastal low to far east, these threats\nwould not be realized. These scenarios will need to be \nmonitored through the weekend and into next week.",
